<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1250–1517 (the Bahri and Burji sultans of Egypt and Syria)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Sultan to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad.jpg" alt="al-Nasir Muhammad"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a><span class="soc-credit">14th century authors · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">From 1250 to 1517 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> were ruled by a military caste of men who had begun life as slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> were bought as boys, mostly Kipchak Turks and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> from the steppes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, trained in the barracks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Citadel of Cairo</a> and then freed. As free men they formed the ruling caste: they chose the sultan, commanded the army as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> and drew the revenues of the land through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> grants. Their own sons, born free and Muslim, were mostly kept out of it. Below the military elite stood the civilian notables, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> and the scribes, then the merchants and craftsmen of the towns and the peasants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley and Syria, who were the great majority. Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived as protected but subordinate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, and slaves served in households across the realm.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1250–1517 (the Bahri and Burji sultans of Egypt and Syria)</span><span><b>People</b> 6 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 10</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The sultan</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:35%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Amirs</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:46%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Mamluk soldiers</span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.1–0.2%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:57%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Sons of mamluks</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:67%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Scholars and judges</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:78%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Officials and scribes</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:89%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-unk" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Townspeople</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:90%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Peasants</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:10%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Christians and Jews</span><span class="soc-bar-s">9–10%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:7"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-unk" href="#rank-10" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/baybars" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/baybars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The sultan <span class="soc-term">sultan, al-malik</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Many Mamluk sultans had been bought as slave boys and trained as soldiers before they rose to the throne.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The sultan ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Citadel of Cairo</a>. Many sultans had begun as mamluks themselves, bought as boys and freed; many others were sons of sultans. A new sultan was chosen by a council of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> and mamluks, who swore loyalty to him; he then took the title al-malik, rode in procession through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and had his name read in the Friday sermon, and from 1261 an Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> kept in Cairo, with no real power, confirmed his authority. The sultan made war, levied taxes, issued decrees, assigned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqtas</a> and appointed the governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and the other provinces, and he owned more mamluks than anyone else: his own royal mamluks were his surest support. Some sultans founded dynasties, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a>’s descendants held the throne for most of a century, but the senior amirs often set up a sultan’s young son as a figurehead, and usurpations and murders were common.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> No fixed rule decided the succession: an amir with enough mamluks behind him could seize the throne, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> did in 1260 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barquq">Barquq</a> in 1382, and a sultan who lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a>’ support could be deposed or killed.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with his household and family.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-shadow-caliphate">Abbasid shadow caliphate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Amirs <span class="soc-term">amir; amir mi'a (of a hundred), amir tablkhana (of forty), amir 'ashara (of ten)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Amirs were the top army commanders, paid with the taxes from lands the sultan assigned to them.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> were the senior mamluks, the commanders and governors of the realm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> graded them as amirs of ten, of forty and of a hundred, by the number of mamluks each kept, and an amir of a hundred could lead a thousand horsemen in battle. Each held an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>, the right to collect the revenues of assigned lands, sized to his rank, and with it he paid and equipped his own corps, a private army; when rival amirs used their corps against each other, civilian life was badly disrupted. Unlike a European fief, an iqta was not hereditary, and the state spread an amir’s holdings across several provinces and short terms. The great offices of the court, from the master of the household (ustadar) to the secretary (dawadar), and the governorships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and the other provinces went to amirs. They built mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and tombs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and endowed them as waqf, partly to provide for their descendants, and they rose and fell with their factions.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A mamluk became an amir by the sultan’s favour after years of service and might climb from ten to forty to a hundred; at the top an amir could make himself sultan. A fall from favour could bring imprisonment, the confiscation of his wealth and death.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: the amirs were the senior part of the mamluk caste, which numbered about 10,000 men around 1293.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">Iqta</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mamluk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>0.1–0.2%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Mamluk soldiers <span class="soc-term">mamluk (pl. mamalik), al-mamalik al-sultaniyya (the royal mamluks)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Mamluks were boys bought as slaves and trained as soldiers, then set free to serve their masters in the army.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The mamluks were the free soldiers of the ruling caste, men who had begun life as slaves. Dealers bought boys from beyond the frontiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, Kipchak Turks from the steppes at first and, from about 1400, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, shipped through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. In the barracks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Citadel of Cairo</a> they were converted to Islam, taught Arabic and the religious sciences, and drilled in horsemanship, archery and swordplay; at the end of their training they were freed. They then served either among the royal mamluks, the sultan’s own regiments and the most privileged troops, or in the corps of an amir, for pay and in time a share of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>. They kept Turkish names and spoke Turkish among themselves, rode horses forbidden to other Muslims, and owed lifelong loyalty to the master who had freed them and to their comrades of the same household. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> bought some 4,000, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a> 6,000–7,000, and by 1293 there were about 10,000.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A mamluk rose by his master’s favour, from trooper to amir and sometimes to sultan; when a new sultan came to power, the mamluks of his predecessors could find themselves pushed aside. His own sons were born free and Muslim, and so were mostly barred from following him into the caste.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 10,000 mamluks, amirs included, in the sultanate by 1293, a modern estimate, among some 6 million people around 1500 and more before the plague: roughly 0.1–0.2 per cent, counting the men alone.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluks</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burji-mamluks">Burji Mamluks</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">4</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Sons of mamluks <span class="soc-term">awlad al-nas</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Sons of mamluks were born free, so most could not join the soldier class; many became scholars instead.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The sons of mamluks, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-nas">awlad al-nas</a>, were the paradox at the heart of the system. Born free and Muslim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, they never passed through the slave market, the barracks and the manumission that made a mamluk, and so they were mostly kept out of the ruling caste their fathers had joined. Some served in the halqa, the corps of non-mamluk cavalry under the sultan, which ranked below the mamluk regiments and declined in the 14th century, and a few rose high: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Nasir Hasan (r. 1347–1351, 1354–1361) promoted awlad al-nas in the army and government, which so angered his own mamluks that they killed him in 1361. Most turned to civilian careers, in trade, the administration and above all scholarship. The historian Ibn Taghribirdi (1411–1470), son of the amir Taghribirdi, studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> and wrote a great chronicle of Egypt and its sultans, al-Nujum al-zahira.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A father’s wealth and connections gave them a start, and a sultan’s son might be set on the throne as a figurehead, but the rank their fathers held could not be inherited.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the sons and grandsons of mamluks.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-nas">Awlad al-nas</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Scholars and judges <span class="soc-term">ulama, qadi al-qudat (chief judge), shaykh</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Muslim scholars called ulama worked as judges and teachers, and people looked to them for guidance.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, the scholars of Islamic law and religion, led the civilian population and stood between it and its rulers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> ended the custom of a single Shafi’i chief judge and appointed a chief qadi for each of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools of law, Shafi’i, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> and Hanbali, though the Shafi’is kept some privileges. Below the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> served deputy judges, preachers, prayer leaders and the teachers of the many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> that sultans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> built and endowed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and until the 15th century a scholar or qadi usually held the post of muhtasib, the inspector of Cairo’s markets. The sultans funded the lodges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders such as the Shadhiliyya, whose shaykhs and saints the common people revered. The ulama owed their posts to the sultan, who could bend them: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> compelled the qadi to rule in his interest, and Ibn Taymiyya, the most prominent scholar of the age, was arrested several times for his teachings.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Learning was open to any free Muslim man, and scholars rose by study, patronage and appointment: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, a newcomer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> chief judge.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the scholars, judges, teachers and Sufi shaykhs.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hajar-al-asqalani">Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">6</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Officials and scribes <span class="soc-term">kuttab (sing. katib), arbab al-aqlam (&quot;men of the pen&quot;)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Scribes wrote the sultan’s letters and kept the government’s accounts, and many of them were Christians.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The sultanate ran on paper. Its scribes (kuttab), the “men of the pen”, staffed the chancery, which drafted the sultan’s letters and decrees, the bureau of the army, which kept the records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqtas</a>, and the treasuries. Their craft demanded fine Arabic, accounting and a mastery of forms, and their manuals show the reach of the bureaucracy: al-Qalqashandi, a clerk of the chancery from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, completed his fourteen-volume manual, the Subh al-A’sha, in 1412, building on the work of the administrator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a>. Many officials were Coptic Christians or converts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and their wealth and influence made them targets: popular pressure drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> from government service at least nine times between the late 13th and mid-15th centuries, though they were usually restored once tensions passed, and many converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, or at least professed it, to keep their posts. From the late 14th century the master of the sultan’s household, an amir, became the chief financial official.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Skill and patronage carried a scribe up the bureaus; for a Coptic scribe, conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was often the price of keeping his career.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the scribes and officials of the chancery, the army bureau and the treasuries.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">7</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Townspeople <span class="soc-term">ʿamma (the common people), tujjar (merchants)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Cairo was one of the biggest cities in the world, full of merchants, shopkeepers and craftspeople.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Merchants, shopkeepers, craftsmen and labourers filled the towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> was the greatest: by 1340 it had close to half a million people, the largest city west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and many lived in rented apartments in multi-storey blocks (rab') built above shops and storerooms. At the top of town society stood the great merchants, among them the Karimi, who brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and other goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> were the hinge of the trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Venetian, Genoese and Catalan merchants bought in their ports. Below them came the shopkeepers and the craftsmen who made textiles, sugar, glass, soap, paper and inlaid brass, watched by the market inspector (muhtasib), and below them porters, water-carriers and beggars. Townspeople bore the taxes and exactions of needy sultans and sometimes rioted against them, and from the 1420s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barsbay">Barsbay</a>’s monopoly of the spice trade enriched the state at the expense of local merchants.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Trade could make a fortune, and the plagues, which left fewer hands, improved the lot of the craftsmen who survived; confiscation, debt or a sultan’s monopoly could ruin a merchant.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the towns’ share: Cairo alone had close to half a million people by 1340, and between 150,000 and 300,000 in the 15th century, after the plagues.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">8</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>Most people (the rest)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Peasants <span class="soc-term">fallahin (sing. fallah)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most people were farmers, and in Egypt their fields were watered by the Nile’s yearly flood.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most of the sultanate’s people were peasants: the fellahin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley and Delta and the villagers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> all farming depended on the Nile flood, and the state controlled the land more closely than in Syria, where most farming was rain-fed. Peasants grew grain and the sugar cane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> on which Egypt’s chief industries depended, and every crop was taxed. The revenues of each village went to the sultan’s treasury or as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqtas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> and soldiers, and holders of short, scattered grants collected the taxes but often neglected the canals and dykes. Land surveys in 1298 and 1315 recorded each village’s fields, their quality and their expected revenue. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> raids destroyed crops and sugar works, and plague returned again and again: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1347–1349 killed about 40 per cent of Egypt’s people, and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a> the taxpayers fell from 6,000 to 116. Many survivors fled to the towns and refused to return to the estates.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Flight to the towns, easier once the plagues had emptied them, was the usual way off the land.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: chiefly the peasants of the Nile valley and the Syrian countryside, the great majority. This count also takes in the amirs, the sons of mamluks, the scholars, the scribes, the townspeople, the Bedouin and the enslaved, whose numbers are unknown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">Iqta</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">9</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>9–10%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Christians and Jews <span class="soc-term">dhimmi, ahl al-dhimma (&quot;people of the pact&quot;)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Christians and Jews could keep their religions but paid a special tax and had to follow extra rules.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, protected but subordinate. Their own religious leaders governed much of their community life, but the state, often invoking the Pact of Umar, set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> poll tax paid by adult men, licensed their houses of worship and regulated their dress and public conduct: in Egyptian towns they might not even ride donkeys. Most Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a>, peasants and townspeople as well as scribes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> had Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Maronite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and Armenian Christians; Jews lived chiefly in the cities. Jews generally fared better than Christians, whom many Muslims linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and resented for their places in the bureaucracy. Hostility came mostly from the streets rather than from the sultans, but in 1301 the government closed the churches, and in 1321 church burnings were followed by fires in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Because conversion lifted the jizya and the restrictions, a great wave of Copts turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 14th century.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> and the restrictions at once and saved a scribe’s career; so many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> took that road that by 1517 Muslims may have outnumbered Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by ten to one.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> By the end of the period Muslims may have outnumbered Christians in Egypt by ten to one, roughly 9 per cent Christian; the share was higher around 1300, before the conversions of the 14th century, and the Jews and the Christians of Syria add more.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">Dhimmi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">Jizya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-10"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">10</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Enslaved people <span class="soc-term">ʿabd (slave), jariya (slave woman), umm walad, tawashi (eunuch)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay, most of them as household servants.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Apart from the boys bought to become mamluks, slaves in the sultanate served mostly in households, and women always outnumbered men among them. Custom kept free Muslim women from working as maids, so slave women cooked, cleaned and served in the homes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a>, scholars and merchants, and some, trained as singers and dancers, became concubines: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> left 1,200 slave women at his death. Slaves reached the markets from many directions: Black Africans across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, Indians across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> and others through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Dealers’ handbooks ranked them by ethnic stereotype, and prices followed: in the late 14th century an Ethiopian girl cost about 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> and a Greek girl up to 550. African men were set to menial work, tending the mamluks’ horses, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> guarded the harems. A slave woman who bore her master a child he acknowledged became an umm walad: the child was free, and she could no longer be sold.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A slave could be freed by an owner or agree a contract to buy his freedom with his own earnings, and an umm walad was freed at her master’s death. Khawand Tughay, bought as a slave by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a>, was freed and married by him.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: slaves served in households across the realm, and women always outnumbered men among them.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuch</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="mamluk-sultanate">Be born in the Mamluk Sultanate of Egypt and Syria</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> system turned the usual ladder upside down: the surest road to power began in a slave market. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, a Kipchak boy captured in a Mongol invasion, was sold at the slave market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> and again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, came into the service of the sultan as-Salih Ayyub and in 1260 seized the throne, which he held until 1277; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barquq">Barquq</a>, a Circassian mamluk of the amir Yalbugha, became sultan in 1382. Yet the military caste was closed by birth to everyone else, the mamluks’ own sons included, who rose instead through learning and office. Among civilians, scholarship and the bureaucracy were the ways up: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, an exile from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, became a chief judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and Coptic scribes kept their careers by converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Women could rise through the harem: Khawand Tughay, bought as a slave, was freed and married by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a>. Falls were sudden, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> lost their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqtas</a> and fortunes to the next faction, and the plagues drove peasants into the towns.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>A woman’s place followed her household’s rank and whether she was free or enslaved. Under Islamic law a free woman could own and inherit property, though a daughter inherited half a son’s share, and she received a dower at marriage. Many women of the ruling elite had been slaves themselves: the consorts of the Bahri sultans began as slave girls, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a>, the widow of the Ayyubid sultan as-Salih Ayyub, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as sultan in 1250 before her marriage to the amir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aybak">Aybak</a>. Khawand Tughay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a>’s favourite wife, had been bought as a slave; at her death in 1348 she owned 1,000 slave women and 80 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and was buried in her own mausoleum. Mothers and widows of sultans could help make or secure a reign, and the Burji sultans often married free women of mamluk families. Enslaved women had no protection from their owners.</p></section>
